From 58b173e3662ea36eac36b5fa4fd342e41a23cb95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: alantero <36408154+alantero@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 05:36:21 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] Show achievement toasts and an achievements list in the
 native menus

The SDK keeps the achievements (XGIUserWriteAchievements, the
AchievementManager and user/achievements/<title>.toml), but its toast is
an ImGui dialog of the presenter, which the native renderer does not
have: an unlock was saved and never shown. The menus' RmlUi now shows
them, in both renderers (CreateAchievementNotificationDialog returns
nothing, so Xenos does not show the SDK's as well).

- A toast for each new unlock, after the Xbox 360 one: a medal pops in at
  the bottom center, opens into a pill with "Achievement unlocked", the
  gamerscore and the name and the achievement's icon, closes and spins
  away. Lights run around the medal's ring, the ring blinks green, and an
  orb and a green trophy take turns in it about once a second. The
  achievement's sound plays with it. Toasts queue and wait while the list
  shows.
- The list (Achievements in the game menu, or the controller's Guide
  button, also during a toast, focused on that achievement): progress and
  gamerscore, every achievement with its icon, description and unlock
  date, filters (all, unlocked, locked), four sorts, and secret
  achievements hidden until unlocked.
- Names, descriptions and icons are the title's (its XDBF), in the game's
  language, and so are the words around them (TextIn), whatever the
  menus' language.
- Every image is made on the CPU at the pixel size it shows at
  (achievement_art): the 64x64 icons resampled with Catmull-Rom and
  rounded, their locked versions, and procedural lights, glow, shine,
  trophy and padlock, since the render interface has no shaders. The orb
  and the sound are optional local files (achievements/orb.png and
  unlock.wav beside the executable or in Resources); without them the
  orb is a neutral sphere and the toast is silent.
- The Guide button reaches the menus even with guide_button off
  (Device::guide); the guest still sees it only with guide_button.
- On macOS GameController hands the Home button to the app in front only
  while the app claims it, and SDL gives the claim back each time a
  controller closes: while the game runs it claims it again on every
  controller (menu/home_button_mac.mm). macOS 26 acts on the press as
  well, and no app can stop it: when the button is released it shows its
  Game Overlay over a game it lists for it, or opens its Games app. So for
  a few seconds after a press the game took, the overlay is hidden (closed
  if it stays in front) and the Games app closed (hidden when it was open
  already), and the game stays in front. The overlay is also what hands
  the button to the system, so the game starts it again when another app
  comes to the front or the game closes.
- NARUTORISE_ACHIEVEMENT_PREVIEW shows toasts, the list or the menu, or
  acts as a Home button press, for checking the presentation; "unlock"
  unlocks one through the SDK.
